{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And the physicians embalmed.</b> Joseph did so for the honor of his father, for so is the way of the honored, and all the more so the great ones of the kingdom. Or, so that they would not be mistaken about him if they did not embalm him — either that he had not died, or that he had died and did not putrefy, and they would make him a god, for he was a wonder among the defiled nations, and this would prevent bringing him up to the land of his burial. And if these reasons just mentioned did not apply, the matter is simple: for even without embalming he would not have putrefied, as you may learn from the incident of Rabbi Elazar son of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai, as mentioned in the Talmud (Bava Metzia 84b)." "Or it is possible to say that since Israel who stood at Mount Sinai had their defilement removed from them (Shabbat 146a), therefore the righteous of Israel from then onward did not putrefy after their death — and even the food residue remaining in the intestines would dry up and become dust, for the defilement of the body is what causes the putrefaction of the food in the intestines, which was not so before Sinai. And although it is said that Jacob's body would not putrefy because he did not die, nonetheless the food residue that remains in a person's body, though gathered up, will nevertheless over time soften and putrefy, and therefore Joseph was careful and commanded to embalm." "<b>Or</b> it is possible that Joseph did not know this sod and therefore commanded to embalm."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 50, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:51:17.870370369Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}